<article class="post-body">

<!-- ───────────────── §01 THE FULL CIRCLE ───────────────── -->
<section id="s-circle" data-code="circle">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Full Circle</div>
<h2>We've been here before</h2>
<p>In 2019, the canonical pipeline was: <em>pre-train on text → fine-tune on labeled task data</em>. BERT and GPT-1 did this, and it worked brilliantly. Pre-trained models outperformed supervised-only baselines by 10–20%. Then GPT-3 arrived and showed something remarkable: if you scale pre-training enough, you don't need fine-tuning at all — models generalize to new tasks with just a few examples shown in context.</p>

<p>That era felt like a breakthrough toward <strong>genuine generalization</strong>. But the benchmarks kept getting harder. AIME, HMMT, SWE-bench, ARC-AGI-2 — tasks requiring expert-level reasoning. LLMs struggled. The community's response? Curate SFT datasets that closely match the benchmarks, then run RL in environments shaped to those benchmarks. Hundreds of billions of tokens of post-training, equivalent to GPT-3's entire pre-training run.</p>

<p>This paper argues we've come full circle — back to the "pre-train then fine-tune" era, but at a scale that obscures what's really happening.</p>

<!-- Timeline viz -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">LLM Training Paradigm Evolution</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click any era to expand</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="era-timeline" id="era-timeline">
      <div class="era-block" data-era="0">
        <div class="era-year">Pre-2017</div>
        <div class="era-name">Supervised Only</div>
        <div class="era-desc">Each task gets its own dataset. Training and test are i.i.d. A model for SQuAD knows nothing about MNLI. Performance = distribution fit.</div>
        <span class="era-tag sup">supervised</span>
      </div>
      <div class="era-block" data-era="1">
        <div class="era-year">2017–2020</div>
        <div class="era-name">Pre-train + Fine-tune</div>
        <div class="era-desc">BERT/GPT-1: self-supervised pre-training → task-specific fine-tuning. Pre-training gives +10–20% over supervised-only. The golden template.</div>
        <span class="era-tag self">self-supervised</span>
      </div>
      <div class="era-block" data-era="2">
        <div class="era-year">2020–2022</div>
        <div class="era-name">Few-Shot Learning</div>
        <div class="era-desc">GPT-3: scale pre-training → zero/few-shot works out of the box. No task-specific training at all. The dream of true generalization.</div>
        <span class="era-tag self">self-supervised</span>
      </div>
      <div class="era-block" data-era="3">
        <div class="era-year">2022–2023</div>
        <div class="era-name">Alignment Post-training</div>
        <div class="era-desc">InstructGPT/RLHF: small SFT + RLHF phase to align format and safety. Lightweight — millions of tokens. Doesn't change what the model knows.</div>
        <span class="era-tag self">self-supervised</span>
      </div>
      <div class="era-block active" data-era="4">
        <div class="era-year">2024–Now</div>
        <div class="era-name">Massive Post-training</div>
        <div class="era-desc">Hundreds of billions of SFT tokens + RL in benchmark-shaped environments. Post-training is now as big as GPT-3's entire pre-training. Benchmarks are pulled in-distribution.</div>
        <span class="era-tag mass">supervised at scale</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="callout amber">
  <p><strong>The parallel:</strong> In the BERT era, the test set was i.i.d. with training because both came from the same dataset. Today the cycle runs in reverse — the benchmark (test set) comes first, then the community curates SFT data and RL environments designed to match it. Same result: training and evaluation distributions converge.</p>
</div>

<p>The difference is scale. Traditional fine-tuning used millions of tokens. Modern post-training uses hundreds of <em>billions</em> — an amount equivalent to GPT-3's complete pre-training run, consumed by what was once a brief alignment step.</p>
</section>

<!-- ───────────────── §02 THE KEY EXPERIMENT ───────────────── -->
<section id="s-experiment" data-code="experiment">
<div class="section-no">02 — The Key Experiment</div>
<h2>Training from random noise — and it works</h2>
<p>If post-training merely <em>unlocks</em> capabilities already learned during pre-training, then a model initialized from random weights should fail completely — it has no language, no mathematics, no prior world knowledge. Conversely, if post-training is an aggressive distribution-fitting mechanism, a scratch-initialized model should achieve non-trivial results on the exact benchmarks the SFT data was curated for.</p>

<p>The paper tests this head-on. Take Qwen-2.5 at 1.5B, 3B, 7B, and 14B. Keep the architecture but throw away the pre-trained weights. Initialize from scratch. Then run the same massive SFT phase — 100B tokens of Open Math Reasoning (OMR) or Open Code Reasoning II (OCR-II) — that transforms base models into state-of-the-art reasoners.</p>

<!-- Before/After canvas -->
<div class="vp">
