<p>The results are striking. The 7B scratch model (zero pre-training) scores <strong>27.1% on competition math</strong> — compared to 0.2% for the base model (no SFT) and 47.9% for the fully pre-trained model with SFT. The scratch model reaches <strong>57% of the fully pre-trained model's performance</strong> despite starting from random noise. On MATH-500, it reaches 79.5 vs. 93.6 — an 85% relative score.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <p><strong>The 8–20% gap mirrors history:</strong> When BERT and GPT-1 were introduced, pre-trained models outperformed supervised-only counterparts by 10–20%. The authors note that today's gap between pre-trained and scratch-SFT is exactly the same magnitude — the same historical signal that originally justified the pre-training paradigm.</p>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ───────────────── §03 DISTRIBUTION FITTING ───────────────── -->
<section id="s-fitting" data-code="fitting">
<div class="section-no">03 — Distribution Fitting</div>
<h2>Post-training pulls benchmarks in-distribution</h2>
<p>Why does a model starting from random weights learn to solve competition math? Because the SFT dataset — OMR with 3.2 million reasoning problems and their solutions — <em>is</em> competition math. The model is not learning to reason; it is learning to pattern-match a distribution of (problem, solution-chain) pairs that tightly overlaps with the evaluation benchmark.</p>

<!-- Distribution animation canvas -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">How Post-training Shifts the Model Distribution</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">press Play to animate</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="min-height:280px">
    <canvas id="dist-canvas" width="600" height="240" style="width:100%;max-width:600px;display:block;margin:0 auto"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.5rem;justify-content:center;margin-top:.875rem">
      <button class="hero-btn" id="dist-play">▶ Play</button>
      <button class="hero-btn" id="dist-reset">↺ Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:.68rem;color:var(--muted);text-align:center;margin:.5rem 0 0">Green = benchmark evaluation distribution. Particles = model's output distribution. Watch as SFT pulls them together.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The "distribution overlap" framing is not metaphorical. The paper tests cross-domain generalization: does a model heavily post-trained on math improve on code? No. The 0%-Pre math model, despite reaching 27% on competition math, scores near-zero on LCB — same as the base model. The SFT distribution is so targeted that it doesn't transfer even to nearby reasoning domains.</p>

<div class="callout cyan">
  <p><strong>Crucially, the same failure occurs in 100%-Pre models:</strong> a fully pre-trained model post-trained exclusively on math doesn't improve on code either. The pre-training doesn't save you — once post-training specializes the distribution, the model is in-distribution for math and out-of-distribution for code, regardless of the pre-training backbone.</p>
</div>

<h3>Modern vs. traditional post-training</h3>
<p>Two differences matter but don't change the fundamental picture:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Scale:</strong> BERT-era fine-tuning used millions of tokens. Modern post-training uses hundreds of billions — equivalent to GPT-3's entire pre-training compute.</li>
  <li><strong>Dynamics:</strong> RL generates training trajectories dynamically from the model's own outputs, rather than from a static dataset. But the signal is still benchmark-shaped reward — it's still fitting the target distribution.</li>
</ul>
</section>

