<!-- ───────────────── §05 MINIMAL PRE-TRAINING ───────────────── -->
<section id="s-minimal" data-code="minimal">
<div class="section-no">05 — 0.1% Pre-training</div>
<h2>A tiny pre-training phase halves the gap</h2>
<p>Beyond the binary of "no pre-training" vs. "full pre-training," the paper tests a minimal setup: pre-train on 10 billion tokens of general text (from DCLM), then run the same SFT phase. Ten billion tokens is less than <strong>0.1%</strong> of Qwen-2.5's 18 trillion pre-training tokens — a fraction so small it's effectively "almost from scratch."</p>

<!-- Scaling chart canvas -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Pre-training Scale vs. Post-SFT Performance (7B Math)</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">competition math avg, pass@1</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="scale-canvas" width="600" height="240" style="width:100%;max-width:600px;display:block;margin:0 auto"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The 0.1%-Pre models close 20–50% of the remaining gap between scratch and full pre-training, across most scales. The 7B 0.1%-Pre-Code model jumps from 42.9% (0%-Pre) to 50.2% on LCB V6 — halving the distance to 56.0% (100%-Pre).</p>

<div class="callout cyan">
  <p><strong>Why does so little pre-training help?</strong> The authors hypothesize that the gain is primarily structural, not knowledge-based. Ten billion tokens of general text provides better weight initialization and teaches the model basic linguistic structure — but doesn't expose it to math or code. The model still acquires all domain-specific capability through SFT. This suggests the performance gap between pre-trained and scratch models is largely about <em>starting-point quality</em>, not what the model "knows."</p>
</div>

<h3>Training dynamics</h3>
<p>The learning curves reveal something interesting. Pre-trained models (100%-Pre) adapt quickly — they capture most of their performance gain in the first half of training. Scratch models start slowly, then show rapid improvement over the first 20,000 steps. By 50,000 steps both have converged, though the pre-trained model stays ahead throughout. The scratch model isn't incapable — it's just starting from a worse initialization.</p>
</section>

<!-- ───────────────── §06 LEARN TO LEARN ───────────────── -->
<section id="s-future" data-code="future">
<div class="section-no">06 — Learn to Learn</div>
<h2>The trap, and how to escape it</h2>
<p>If post-training is just massive supervised learning, the field is trapped in a <strong>reactive cycle</strong>: a new hard benchmark appears, the community curates SFT data and RL environments matching it, models saturate the benchmark within a year, and a new harder benchmark appears. Repeat indefinitely.</p>

<!-- Step navigator for the cycle -->
<div class="step-list" id="cycle-steps">
  <div class="step-box active" data-step="0">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">New hard benchmark introduced</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Community creates a benchmark where initial model performance is far below human level. E.g. AIME (initial GPT-4: ~5%), ARC-AGI-2 (frontier models: ~4%).</div>
      <div class="step-code">initial_accuracy ≈ 5% (far below human)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="1">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">SFT data & RL environments curated</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Teams mine, synthesize, and distill analogous training examples from other models. 100B+ tokens of SFT data engineered to match benchmark distribution.</div>
      <div class="step-code">sft_data ← mine_similar(benchmark)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="2">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Benchmark saturated ≈ 1 year later</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Models post-trained on the curated distribution reach near-human or superhuman performance. The benchmark is declared "solved." But this is distribution fit, not generalization.</div>
      <div class="step-code">final_accuracy ≈ 85%+ (benchmark "solved")</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="3">
    <div class="step-num">4</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">New benchmark required — cycle repeats</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Models fail on new out-of-distribution tasks. Another benchmark is introduced. Another massive post-training cycle begins. Generalization beyond the training distribution remains elusive.</div>
      <div class="step-code">→ goto step 1</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="cycle-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
  <button id="cycle-next">Next →</button>
  <span class="step-progress" id="cycle-prog">Step 1 of 4</span>
</div>

<h3>The proposed exit: Learn to Learn</h3>
<p>The paper's prescription is meta-learning: rather than post-training models to solve known tasks, train them to <em>acquire</em> new capabilities autonomously. When an LLM system encounters a task it cannot solve, it should be able to self-adapt — searching for relevant SFT data, engineering a reward signal, updating its own weights — without human intervention.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <p><strong>The key distinction:</strong> Current post-training is a <em>one-time</em> procedure performed by researchers before deployment. "Learn to learn" would make adaptation a <em>continuous</em> process performed by the model itself post-deployment. Once a model can self-evolve, it can theoretically improve indefinitely on any task it encounters — limited only by compute, not by what researchers pre-anticipated needing.</p>
</div>

<p>This vision doesn't prescribe a specific mechanism. It could be self-modifying prompts and code. It could be running RL against proxy reward signals the model itself engineers. It could involve alternative architectures that support effective in-context adaptation without massive supervised training. The unifying principle is the same: the model must learn how to learn, not just what to memorize.</p>

<p>Whether current transformer architectures support this, or whether new architectural primitives are needed, remains an open question — and one the paper deliberately leaves open. What it doesn't leave open is the diagnosis: the current paradigm is not heading toward general intelligence. It is heading toward faster distribution-fitting.</p>
</section>

